<edge source="russia_ukraine_warfare::sanctions_circumvention" target="brics_economic_realignme::crossborder_capital_vol" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">An increased ability to circumvent sanctions expands use of opaque intermediary channels and nonstandard payment routes, producing episodic flows, rapid reversals and pricing dislocations that raise short-term cross-border capital flow volatility.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_warfare</data>
  <data key="d40">brics_economic_realignme</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_warfare::sanctions_circumvention" target="red_sea_hormuz_crisis::chokepoint_attack_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Improved capacity to evade sanctions (e.g., covert ship‑to‑ship transfers, reflagging, masking AIS) increases clandestine traffic through chokepoints and provokes interdiction attempts and confrontations that raise the frequency of attacks and incidents at those chokepoints.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_warfare</data>
  <data key="d40">red_sea_hormuz_crisis</data>
</edge>
