<edge source="global_rate_market_shock::central_bank_policy_rate" target="brics_economic_realignme::corporate_outbound_inves" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">A persistent increase in the official policy rate raises corporate borrowing costs and tends to appreciate the domestic currency, reducing firms' incentives and financial capacity to finance M&amp;A, greenfield projects or other outbound investment.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_rate_market_shock</data>
  <data key="d40">brics_economic_realignme</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_rate_market_shock::central_bank_policy_rate" target="russia_ukraine_warfare::energy_price_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">A rapid increase in policy rates suppresses demand growth and strengthens safe‑haven currencies (reducing commodity purchasing power), depressing spot and forward energy prices in global markets.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_rate_market_shock</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_warfare</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_rate_market_shock::central_bank_policy_rate" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::model_deployment_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">An increase in official policy rates raises corporate discount rates and the cost of financing operational deployments, prompting firms to postpone or slow rollout of new models to conserve cash and reduce risk exposure.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_rate_market_shock</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
