<edge source="global_rate_market_shock::policy_rate_expectations" target="brics_economic_realignme::crossborder_capital_vol" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">A sudden upward revision in expected policy rates triggers accelerated portfolio rebalancing and stop-start flows as global investors reduce duration and shift liquidity across borders, increasing short‑term cross‑border capital flow volatility.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_rate_market_shock</data>
  <data key="d40">brics_economic_realignme</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_rate_market_shock::policy_rate_expectations" target="red_sea_hormuz_crisis::financial_market_volatil" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Upward revisions to expected future policy rates provoke rapid repricing of asset valuations and risk premia, triggering higher realized and implied volatility across FX, rates and commodity markets.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_rate_market_shock</data>
  <data key="d40">red_sea_hormuz_crisis</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_rate_market_shock::policy_rate_expectations" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::crossborder_competition" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Stronger market expectations of future rate hikes increase anticipated financing and FX costs and raise uncertainty for cross-border M&amp;A and expansion, reducing the pace and intensity of US–China competitive investments and strategic market entries.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_rate_market_shock</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
